(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-clinical-questions-reviews-quiz"]={"title":"Clinical Questions and Reviews Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why should publication bias always be considered when reading a review?","choices":["Missing studies can distort the evidence","It affects only narrative reviews","A random effects model corrects it","Two reviewers selecting studies removes it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a review can only include what has been published, so unpublished null results leave it looking at a skewed set of studies. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"To which kinds of questions is grading of evidence most often applied?","choices":["Prevalence and incidence","Etiology and disease naming","Anatomy and staging","Treatment and screening"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and grading is a quick way to assess clinical significance, although it still involves some value judgment in weighing pros and cons. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does a grade of 2A imply for the best action?","choices":["It is the same for every patient","It may vary with values","It rests on low-quality evidence","It should never be considered"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the best action may differ with circumstances or values: a weak recommendation with high-quality evidence reflects benefits closely balanced with risks and burdens. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What happens after peer reviewers recommend acceptance?","choices":["It is published with no further review","The authors repeat the study","Editors review language and grammar","Peer reviewers correct the grammar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and only then is the article published; peer review as a whole helps to counter conflict of interest. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"When does the grading scheme give a weak recommendation?","choices":["Benefits are closely balanced with risks","Risks clearly outweigh benefits","Benefits clearly outweigh burdens","Every study reports the same effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and uncertainty in the estimates of benefits, risks and burdens can also lead to a weak grade. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which standard requires disclosure of members' financial, intellectual, institutional and patient or public interests?","choices":["Transparency","External review","Conflict of interest","Updating"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because group members' conflicts related to financial, intellectual, institutional and patient or public activities bearing on the guideline are disclosed. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which question is asked when reading beyond the abstract?","choices":["How strong were the methods?","What is the study about?","What was concluded?","Who does it apply to?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the reader also asks how big the effect was, what limitations and biases existed, and what the author recommends. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why should conflicts of interest be acknowledged?","choices":["So the study is excluded automatically","So readers can judge whether bias is present","So that the findings become significant","So peer review becomes unnecessary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because bias may be unintended or otherwise, and disclosure lets readers weigh how much to trust the results. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A clinician wants one summary of a large body of studies to guide a decision. Which is helpful?","choices":["A narrative review","A systematic review","A single case report","An expert's personal opinion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a systematic review summarizes bodies of evidence so clinicians can make decisions; good ones provide the highest level of evidence. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A systematic review searches only published articles. Which studies is it most likely to be missing?","choices":["Studies with significant results","Studies with rigorous methods","Studies with very large samples","Studies without significant results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it happens through editor preference and sponsors who are less willing to support research unfavorable to their product. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which question belongs to quick reading of an abstract?","choices":["How big was the effect?","What limitations existed and were they addressed?","Are the conclusions valid?","What impact will the results have?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, along with what the study is about, who it applies to, and whether it can and should be applied to your patients. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"The title and keywords of an article match the topic sought. What comes next when screening?","choices":["Read the entire article","Read the abstract and conclusion","Skip to the next article","Check the reference list first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the reader checks for clear aims, a well-defined hypothesis and precise conclusions before deciding whether to read on. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which two statistical tests are named for assessing heterogeneity?","choices":["Cochran's Q and the I-squared statistic","Kaplan-Meier and log-rank tests","Pearson and Spearman correlation","Mann-Whitney and Wilcoxon tests"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both quantify how much study results differ, which guides whether and how the results can be pooled. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which grade is a strong recommendation supported by high-quality evidence?","choices":["1A","1C","2A","2C"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the benefits clearly outweigh risks and burdens, and the evidence is consistent, from well-performed randomized controlled trials. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the purpose of grading evidence?","choices":["A replacement for clinical judgment","A quick way to assess clinical significance","A guarantee that a treatment works","A measure of statistical significance alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though some value judgment remains, because the pros and cons still have to be weighed. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which strength is typical of a narrative review?","choices":["It answers one predetermined clinical question","It applies explicit rules for choosing studies","It produces a pooled numerical estimate of effect","It can cover broad topics and many issues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because an expert summary is not tied to a single question, so it can range across a whole disease and the issues around it. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of these is a suggested way to keep current?","choices":["Sales representatives","Advertising","Patients","Press releases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the others suggested are professional organizations, journal articles and colleagues. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What should a systematic review list for each excluded study?","choices":["The reason it was excluded","The reviewer's personal opinion of it","The date each study was rejected","The name of the reviewer who disagreed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because listing excluded studies together with the rationale lets a reader judge whether the criteria were applied fairly. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is the pooled summary shown on a forest plot?","choices":["As a diamond","As a pyramid","As a funnel","As a circle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the diamond marks the summary effect from pooling, shown as the summary of the pooled studies. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How are the studies in a systematic review summarized?","choices":["Scatter plots and life tables","Nomograms and receiver operating characteristic curves","Funnel plots and dose-response curves","Forest plots and tables of characteristics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because figures such as forest plots show results across studies, and tables set out the characteristics of each one. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which topic suits a narrative review better than a systematic review?","choices":["Whether one drug lowers mortality more than another","Whether a new test detects a disease more accurately","Whether screening lowers deaths in one age group","An overview of many aspects of a disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because narrative reviews suit broad topics and a range of issues, whereas a systematic review needs a focused question. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which is a way conflict of interest can manifest?","choices":["Random assignment of participants","Calculation of confidence intervals","Blinding of outcome assessors","Selective reporting of results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and other manifestations are the choice of studies in reviews and meta-analyses, and exaggeration or fabrication of results. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A meta-analysis combines one high-quality trial with several seriously flawed studies. Which appraisal question does this raise?","choices":["Was a forest plot drawn for the review?","Are the studies all of equal quality?","Did the reviewers write a protocol first?","Were the excluded studies all listed?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the quality of the included studies should be examined, and mixing very different levels of quality weakens the pooled result. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which question contains all four components of a well-built clinical question?","choices":["In adults with migraine, does daily propranolol reduce the number of headache days?","Does daily propranolol, compared with placebo, work better than other treatments?","In adults with migraine, does daily propranolol, compared with placebo, reduce headache days?","In adults with migraine, is propranolol better, compared with placebo, in some way?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because it names the patients (adults with migraine), intervention (propranolol), comparison (placebo) and outcome (headache days). — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which methods explore reasons for differences among studies?","choices":["Subgroup analysis, sensitivity analysis and metaregression","Blinding, randomization and allocation concealment","Fixed weighting, rounding and narrative summary","Forest plots, nomograms and receiver operating characteristic curves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and subgroups can be based on study quality and other factors; the text should also discuss the role of bias and study factors. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why are authors of narrative reviews often well placed to write them?","choices":["They follow a written protocol set in advance","They are free of financial or personal influence","They usually treat patients with the disease","They have read every published study on the topic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because authors of narrative reviews usually have experience caring for patients with the disease, which lends the summary clinical perspective. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A reviewer leaves out a trial with only a handful of participants. Which question about sampling the best studies guided this?","choices":["Was the research peer-reviewed?","Was the sample size sufficient?","Is the information still current?","What were the limitations?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a study too small to give a reliable estimate is weighed against the others when the best studies are sampled. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A trade group's guideline endorses only products sold by its member companies. Which conflict of interest is this?","choices":["Financial conflict of interest","Institutional conflict","Personal conflict of interest","Intellectual conflict of interest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because institutional conflict arises when a recommendation benefits one organization more than others. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does publication bias differ from reporting bias?","choices":["Reporting bias concerns whole studies not being published","Both concern errors in how participants were assigned","Publication bias concerns whole studies not being published","Both are removed by a larger sample size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because non-significant research is less likely to be published, whereas reporting bias concerns only a subset of a study's data being made available. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What do the letters A, B and C in a recommendation grade indicate?","choices":["The strength of the recommendation","The size of the patient group","The cost of the treatment","The quality of the supporting evidence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the letters express confidence in the estimate of effect, with A the highest, based on the type and quality of studies behind it. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"}]};
